WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.681
A practical Cyber Kill Chain routine starts with scope and purpose.

2
00:00:04.831 --> 00:00:12.490
Decide whether the team is reviewing an incident, planning detections, evaluating controls, preparing an exercise, or summarizing intelligence.

3
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.172
Then collect evidence and place observed behavior into the stage that best fits.

4
00:00:18.322 --> 00:00:24.280
Use careful language: observed, suspected, inferred, and unknown should not mean the same thing.

5
00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:29.536
Record confidence and assumptions so later readers understand how conclusions were reached.

6
00:00:29.686 --> 00:00:36.069
For each stage, ask what could detect, deny, disrupt, degrade, deceive, or contain the activity.

7
00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:40.900
Look for telemetry gaps, prevention gaps, response gaps, and recovery gaps.

8
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:47.007
Translate those gaps into improvements with owners: a new log source, a refined alert,

9
00:00:47.157 --> 00:00:53.966
a hardened exposed service, a tested containment path, a clearer playbook, or a better executive summary.

10
00:00:54.116 --> 00:00:57.520
The routine should produce decisions, not just labels.

11
00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:04.053
It should help defenders communicate what happened, what evidence supports the assessment, what risk remains,

12
00:01:04.203 --> 00:01:06.403
and what will be improved.

13
00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:15.064
The core lesson is simple: the Cyber Kill Chain is valuable when it turns intrusion progression into practical defensive action.

14
00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:18.618
Use the routine after the incident as well.

15
00:01:18.768 --> 00:01:27.279
Revisit the mapped stages, compare expected evidence to actual evidence, and ask where the team depended on luck or manual

16
00:01:27.429 --> 00:01:29.629
effort.

17
00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:35.311
Those observations can become durable improvements to detections, controls, playbooks, exercises, and reporting.
